WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.820
Why are so many animals brown or green while others stand out?

2
00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:09.000
In this thirty-minute game, your child plays a bird hunting yarn worms.

3
00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:13.740
You can see which colors are harder to find when you change the background.

4
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.080
What does your child get out of this exercise?

5
00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.540
They practice making a prediction before testing it.

6
00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:28.500
They look closely across a busy patch and sort their finds by color.

7
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.040
Then they check what changes on a new background.

8
00:00:33.183 --> 00:00:37.883
Cut the yarn yourself into twenty pieces, each about five centimeters long.

9
00:00:39.183 --> 00:00:42.583
Make five green, five brown, five red, and five yellow.

10
00:00:44.023 --> 00:00:46.583
Mark out a lawn patch about two meters on each side.

11
00:00:47.863 --> 00:00:49.823
Use four shoes or short, blunt sticks.

12
00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:54.317
Stay with your child and check the patch for hazards first.

13
00:00:55.637 --> 00:00:56.997
Choose a spot away from roads.

14
00:00:58.177 --> 00:01:00.477
Hunt in shoes and use only soft yarn.

15
00:01:01.797 --> 00:01:03.617
Keep it away from children under four.

16
00:01:04.837 --> 00:01:06.397
Call nine one one if choking.

17
00:01:07.717 --> 00:01:11.657
Keep pets indoors, find all twenty pieces, and wash hands afterward.

18
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.140
Show your child one worm of each color.

19
00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:19.060
Ask, "If you were a bird, which color would you find first?"

20
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:20.640
Write down their guess.

21
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:25.300
While they turn away, scatter all twenty worms on top of the grass.

22
00:01:26.383 --> 00:01:32.683
Say go. Give your child thirty seconds to put worms in the cup, one at a time.

23
00:01:33.903 --> 00:01:35.863
Red and yellow usually go first.

24
00:01:37.183 --> 00:01:38.743
Green and brown are often left.

25
00:01:39.963 --> 00:01:43.223
Record the count for each color and recover every piece.

26
00:01:44.463 --> 00:01:47.103
Play three rounds, then total the counts by color.

27
00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:51.450
For the next hunt, choose bare dirt or a patterned rug.

28
00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:56.770
Before you scatter the worms, ask your child, "Which color do you think will hide

29
00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:58.650
best here?" Write down their guess.

30
00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:01.850
Take a moment to make your own prediction.

31
00:02:07.533 --> 00:02:11.053
Scatter the same worms and run another thirty-second hunt.

32
00:02:12.433 --> 00:02:15.033
Count by color and find every leftover worm.

33
00:02:16.353 --> 00:02:17.353
Check your guesses.

34
00:02:18.553 --> 00:02:21.293
Which colors were found least often on each background?

35
00:02:22.793 --> 00:02:24.813
Brown often hides well on brown dirt.

36
00:02:26.073 --> 00:02:29.553
When a color blends into the background, it can be harder to spot.

37
00:02:30.667 --> 00:02:34.847
Close by asking, "Which worms would still be alive at the end of the day?"

38
00:02:35.647 --> 00:02:38.187
Real animals can pass their colors to their young.

39
00:02:39.427 --> 00:02:44.687
If animals with a matching color survive more often and have more young, that color

40
00:02:44.687 --> 00:02:45.787
can become more common.
